{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2512.05742, how does the AI Act distinguish between scientific research and development and product-oriented research, and what are the implications for internal deployment exemptions?", "answer": "The AI Act differentiates scientific R&D from product-oriented R&D, exempting only AI models or systems developed and deployed internally for the sole purpose of scientific R&D from its scope.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "The AI Act differentiates between scientific R&D and product-oriented R&D (Article 2(6) and Recital 25 AI Act)."}, {"page": 26, "quote": "it is possible to infer from Recital 25 and the REACH Regulation that scientific R&D is different from 'product-oriented' R&D. By contrast to purely scientific R&D, any AI model or system that engages in R&D that is product-oriented does not fall within the Article 2(6) exception."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2512.05742, what two parameters from the REACH Regulation are used to distinguish scientific research and development from product-oriented research and development in the AI Act?", "answer": "The REACH Regulation uses scale (e.g., laboratory scale, controlled conditions) and purpose (experimentation, analysis vs. product development) to distinguish scientific from product-oriented R&D.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "the REACH Regulation appears to differentiate product and process orientated research and development and scientific research and development through a combination of two main parameters. 135 First, there is the scale of the R&D activity. Scientific research and development involves quantities 'less than 1 tonne per year', 'under controlled conditions', and 'at a laboratory scale' (such as 'in vitro diagnostics'), 136 whereas product and process orientated research and development can be either 'in a pilot plant' or in 'full-scale production'. 137"}, {"page": 19, "quote": "Second, there is the purpose of the R&D activity. product and process orientated research and development aims at 'product development or the further development of a substance', 139 whereas scientific research and development aims at 'experimentation, analysis', 140 'monitoring and […] diagnostics'. 141"}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2512.05742, what does the blue box in Figure 1 represent regarding the AI Act's scope?", "answer": "The blue box represents activities preceding internal deployment and AI models or systems specifically developed and deployed for the sole purpose of scientific R&D, which are arguably outside the AI Act's scope.", "evidence": [{"page": 25, "quote": "In blue: the activities preceding internal deployment 181 and AI models or systems specifically developed and deployed for the sole purpose of scientific R&D, 182 arguably falling outside the scope of the AI Act under Article 2(8) and Article 2(6) respectively."}]},
  {"question": "What does 'GPAI model' refer to in arXiv 2512.05742, and when does the AI Act apply to it?", "answer": "'GPAI model' refers to a general-purpose AI model, and the AI Act applies once it is integrated into an AI system that is put into service or placed on the market.", "evidence": [{"page": 26, "quote": "GPAI models trigger the application of the AI Act once they are integrated into an AI system (whether internal or external) that is put into service or once they are independently placed on the market. 184"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2512.05742, what is an example of scientific AI research that is not product-oriented, described without using the phrase 'model organism'?", "answer": "Training AI systems in controlled environments to demonstrate specific failure modes that could pose existential risks, detached from direct product enhancement, is an example of non-product-oriented scientific AI research.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "Model organisms are, literally, 'in vitro demonstrations of the kinds of failures that might pose existential threats'."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2512.05742, what is a research activity that occurs before an AI system is put into service and may be exempt under Article 2(8)?", "answer": "Experimentation and planning around model architectures, training algorithms, and datasets are pre-deployment activities that may be exempt under Article 2(8).", "evidence": [{"page": 23, "quote": "the typical AI development cycle includes (i) experimentation and planning (for instance, around model architectures, training algorithms, datasets), (ii) pre-training to develop a base model, and (iii) posttraining techniques to improve the base model (for instance, fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, and safety training). 171"}]}
]}
